{"name":"Planet Fever","hex":"#af313a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"planet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Each of the seven major bodies which move relative to the fixed stars in the night sky—the Moon, Mercury, Venus, the Sun, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any body that orbits the Sun, including the asteroids (as minor planets) and sometimes the moons of those bodies (as satellite planets)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/planet"},{"term":"fever","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A higher than normal body temperature of a person (or, generally, a mammal), usually caused by disease.","Any of various diseases."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To put into a fever; to affect with fever.","To become fevered."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fever"}],"entries":[{"title":"Planet","blocks":[{"text":"A planet is a large, rounded astronomical body that is generally required to be in orbit around a star, stellar remnant, or brown dwarf, and is not one itself. The Solar System has eight planets by the most restrictive definition of the term: the terrestrial planets Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars, and the giant planets Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune. The best available theory of planet formation is the nebular hypothesis, which posits that an interstellar cloud collapses out of a nebula to create a young protostar orbited by a protoplanetary disk. Planets grow in this disk by the gradual accumulation of material driven by gravity, a process called accretion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Planet_collage_to_scale.jpg/330px-Planet_collage_to_scale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planet","wikidata":"Q634"},{"title":"Fever","blocks":[{"text":"Fever or pyrexia in humans is a symptom of an anti-infection defense mechanism that appears with body temperature exceeding the normal range caused by an increase in the body's temperature set point in the hypothalamus. There is no single agreed-upon upper limit for normal temperature: sources use values ranging between 37.2 and 38.3 °C in humans."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Symptoms-fever.jpg/330px-Symptoms-fever.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fever","wikidata":"Q38933"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg/500px-The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg/1280px-The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg/500px-The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/PIA18469-AsteroidCollision-NearStarNGC2547-ID8-2013.jpg/500px-PIA18469-AsteroidCollision-NearStarNGC2547-ID8-2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/PIA18469-AsteroidCollision-NearStarNGC2547-ID8-2013.jpg/1280px-PIA18469-AsteroidCollision-NearStarNGC2547-ID8-2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/PIA18469-AsteroidCollision-NearStarNGC2547-ID8-2013.jpg/500px-PIA18469-Astero